ce it isn't in the header, but it *is* part of the exported ABI, so I translated it as a public symbol rather than an internal helper.\n\n## Files written\n\n- `translation/Cargo.toml` — `crate-type = [\"cdylib\"]`, `lib name = \"driver\"` (produces `libdriver.so`)\n- `translation/src/lib.rs` — both functions as `#[unsafe(no_mangle)] pub extern \"C\"` taking `c_char`\n\n## Two C behaviors deliberately preserved (not \"fixed\")\n\n1. **Sign-extension in `printf(\"%02x\\n\", charHex)`** — `charHex` is promoted `char`→`int`, then `%x` reinterprets it as `unsigned int`. On x86-64 Linux (signed `char`) negative values print as eight hex digits, so the `%02x` width is irrelevant. `driver(0x7f)` prints `ffffff80`, not `80`. I call libc's `printf` directly through a variadic `extern \"C\"` declaration rather than using Rust's `println!`, which keeps both the byte output *and* the stdio buffering/flush-at-exit timing identical.\n2. **`char result = data + 1` overflow** — reproduced with `wrapping_add`, matching GCC's two's-complement truncation, so `driver(0x7f)` yields `result == -128` and `driver(0xff)` wraps to `00`.\n\n## Completion gate\n\n- `cargo build --release` — clean, no warnings.\n- **Symbol diff empty in both directions**: `comm` of the sorted `nm -D --defined-only` output for the C and Rust `.so` shows no symbols missing from Rust and no extras.\n- **Differential output test**: a `dlopen`-based harness invoked both exports over the entire input domain (all 256 `char` values × 2 functions = 512 lines) against each library. Output is byte-identical — matching md5 `fe71dd18…` and 3072 bytes each.\n\n`c_src/` was not modified (checksums recorded above); my C build scratch directory has been removed.","ttft_ms":2461,"ttft_stream_ms":2292,"time_to_request_ms":51,"type":"result","duration_ms":107761,"uuid":"3b7a31ca-d84f-490c-9202-9487461ac31b","queued_turn_count":0}
